  "text": "**Screening the four solutions**\n\n- **Solution 2 (2/3):** Rejected. It answers a Sidon-set extremal question, not a question about subperiodic group isomorphism classes.\n- **Solution 3 (61, 68):** Rejected. Its layer count of 68 exceeds the upper bound below. It also gives no derivation.\n- **Solution 4 (57, 63):** Rejected. Its layer count of 63 also exceeds that bound, and it cites no derivation.\n- **Solution 1 (32, 34):** Its layer count checks out. Each layer group G projects to a 2D isometry group ρ(G), which is one of the 17 wallpaper groups. The kernel of ρ is either trivial or the horizontal mirror σ_h. In the trivial case G ≅ ρ(G). In the σ_h case G ≅ ρ(G) × Z₂. Wallpaper groups have no nontrivial finite normal subgroup and are pairwise non-isomorphic, so W × Z₂ is never isomorphic to a wallpaper group, and W × Z₂ ≅ W′ × Z₂ forces W ≅ W′. That gives exactly 17 + 17 = 34 classes. The rod count of 32 is plausible but I did not verify its case analysis, so it is the less certain half of the pair.\n\nSolution 1 is the only candidate that survives the layer check, and nothing rules out its rod count. It is not fully verified, so my confidence is moderate.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
